## The one rule that outranks the rest

**Stop before anything is copied.** You may sort, group, look at photos and
write a plan. You may not copy a single file to the drive. Concretely: never
run `photo_execute.py`, and never pass `--go` to anything. The allowlist this
session runs under does not include the copy stage, so an attempt will simply
be refused — that refusal is the design working, not an error to route around.
Do not try another path to the same effect.

The reason is on record: this project's own evaluation judgement is that
folder naming is not yet trustworthy unattended. A folder created at 3am with
a name nobody read is the failure this whole job exists to prevent. The owner
reads the plan in the morning and copies it themselves.

## Classify rules — not negotiable

- The model is pinned to **{{MODEL}}** and you are already running as it. Do
  not switch models, and do not delegate the looking to a smaller one. A
  weaker model once described photos it had never seen; that is why this is
  policy and not a preference.
- Every label you write from an image you actually looked at carries the
  `viewed-image:` provenance prefix. Never put that prefix on a guess, on a
  filename inference, or on a propagated neighbour label.
- After classify and **before** you render any plan, run:
  `{{PYTHON}} {{SCRIPTS}}/photo_classify_validate.py <work dir>`
  A plan rendered on an unvalidated classify is not a plan the owner can
  review, it is a rendered guess. If validation fails, stop there and report
  the failure instead of planning on top of it.

## Steps

1. `{{PYTHON}} {{SCRIPTS}}/photo_run.py status <dump>` — see what is already
   done. This job is resumable; pick up where the last night stopped.
2. For a dump with no `batches.json` yet:
   `{{PYTHON}} {{SCRIPTS}}/photo_run.py prep "<source dump folder>"`
3. Sample and look at each unclassified batch, then write its type/where with
   `photo_classify_set.py`. This is the only step that uses the vision model.
4. Run `photo_classify_validate.py` (see above).
5. Author `plans.json` — group batches, choose destination folder names.
6. Render each plan so a human can read it, using `photo_plan.py`. **Dry-run
   only.** Do not call `photo_run.py plan` or `photo_run.py finish`; those
   take a `--go` you must never pass, so use the plan renderer directly.
7. **Stop.** Do not copy.

If the owner's pack is new and empty, some steps legitimately cannot run yet —
a blank pack may have no folder-type vocabulary to classify into. That is not
a failure to work around: report which step could not run and why, and stop.

## Report

Write a short plain-language summary as the last thing you do. It must say:

- which dump you worked on, and what you finished;
- **what is waiting for the owner** — how many plans are rendered and where to
  read them, plus any memory-review page or unconfirmed screen size;
- the exact command to run to copy, once they are happy with the plan;
- anything that failed or that you refused to do, stated plainly.

